<reading-questions xml:id="reflections-col-row-null-space">
<title> Reflect on your understanding </title>

<introduction><p>
	In addition to the reflection activities below,
	re-read <xref ref="section-col-row-null-space-terminology" text="type-global-title" />.
	Be sure you understand each of the new definitions introduced in this chapter,
	and spend some time committing them to memory.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>
	<title> Domain of a column space </title>
	<p>
	The column space of an <m> m \times n </m> matrix is always a subspace of <m> \R^k </m> for some specific value of <m> k </m>.
	What determines the value of <m> k </m>?
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Column space versus row equivalence </title>
	<p>
	Are the column spaces of <xref ref="definition-row-red-row-equiv" text="title" /> always the same space or sometimes different spaces?
	Explain.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Row equivalence versus column dependence </title>

	<task><p>
		If <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> are <xref ref="definition-row-red-row-equiv" text="title" />,
		are the relationships of linear dependence and independence between columns of <m> A </m> always the same as or sometimes different from
		the relationships of linear dependence and independence between the corresponding columns of <m> B </m>?
	</p></task>

	<task><p> Explain how to use row reduction to test a set of vectors for independence. </p></task>

	<task><p> Explain how to use row reduction to determine an expression for one vector as a linear combination of a set of vectors. </p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Column space basis procedure </title>
	<introduction><p> For a matrix <m> A </m>: </p></introduction>

	<task><p> State the connection between the pattern of positions of leading ones in <m> \RREF(A) </m> and the choice of basis vectors for the column space of <m> A </m>. </p></task>

	<task><p> Explain why we choose our column space basis vectors from amongst the columns of <m> A </m> and not <m> \RREF(A) </m>. </p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Domain of a row space </title>
	<p>
	The row space of an <m> m \times n </m> matrix is always a subspace of <m> \R^k </m> for some specific value of <m> k </m>.
	What determines the value of <m> k </m>?
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Row space versus row equivalence </title>
	<p>
	Are the row spaces of <xref ref="definition-row-red-row-equiv" text="title" /> always the same space or sometimes different spaces?
	Explain.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Row equivalence versus row dependence </title>
	<p>
	If <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> are <xref ref="definition-row-red-row-equiv" text="title" />,
	are the relationships of linear dependence and independence between rows of <m> A </m> always the same as or sometimes different from
	the relationships of linear dependence and independence between the corresponding rows of <m> B </m>?
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Row independence in RREF </title>
	<p> Explain why the pattern of leading ones in an RREF matrix always guarantees that the nonzero rows form a linearly independent set. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Row space basis procedure </title>
	<introduction><p> For a matrix <m> A </m>: </p></introduction>

	<task><p> State the connection between the pattern of positions of leading ones in <m> \RREF(A) </m> and the choice of basis vectors for the row space of <m> A </m>. </p></task>

	<task><p> Explain why we choose our row space basis vectors from amongst the rows of <m> \RREF(A) </m> and not <m> A </m>. </p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Comparison of spanning sets via row reduction </title>
	<p> Explain how to use row reduction to determine whether two sets of vectors span the same space. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Column space procedure versus row space procedure </title>
	<p>
	Given a spanning set for a vector space,
	explain the relative advantages of each of the column space procedure and the row space procedure in obtaining a basis for the space.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Domain of a null space </title>
	<p>
	The null space of an <m> m \times n </m> matrix is always a subspace of <m> \R^k </m> for some specific value of <m> k </m>.
	What determines the value of <m> k </m>?
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Null space versus row equivalence </title>
	<p>
	Are the null spaces of <xref ref="definition-row-red-row-equiv" text="title" /> always the same space or sometimes different spaces?
	Explain.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Null space procedure </title>
	<p> Explain the procedure to determine a basis for the null space of a matrix from parametric equations expressing the general solution to the associated homogeneous system. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title> Dimensions of the three spaces </title>
	<p>
	For each of the column, row, and null spaces,
	explain the connection between the rank of a matrix and the dimension of that space.
	</p>
</exercise>


</reading-questions>
